      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "81573e7f-b328-50f9-8504-840b207f22a1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Joel Chandler Harris, James Baskett\n- Products: Song of the South\n- Characters: Uncle Remus, Br'er Rabbit, Tar-Baby, Br'er Fox\n- Companies: The Walt Disney Company\n- Topics: Uncle Remus stories\n\nFacts:\n- The Tar-Baby was the second Uncle Remus story published in 1880.\n- The Tar-Baby story is about a doll made of tar and turpentine used by Br'er Fox to entrap Br'er Rabbit.\n- Song of the South is based on the collection of Uncle Remus stories as adapted by Joel Chandler Harris.\n- The Walt Disney Company adapted Br'er Rabbit for its 1946 animated motion picture Song of the South.\n- James Baskett was an American actor born on February 16, 1904, and he portrayed Uncle Remus in Song of the South.\n- James Baskett was known for singing \"Zip-a-Dee-Doo-Dah\" in the 1946 Disney feature film Song of the South.\n- James Baskett died on July 9, 1948.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "25fb435a-100e-5317-a303-d5258e08244c",
        "text": "Page: Song of the South. Sentence: It is based on the collection of Uncle Remus stories as adapted by Joel Chandler Harris, and stars James Baskett as Uncle Remus.\nPage: James Baskett. Sentence: James Baskett (February 16, 1904 – July 9, 1948) was an American actor known for his portrayal of Uncle Remus, singing the song \"Zip-a-Dee-Doo-Dah\" in the 1946 Disney feature film \"Song of the South\".\nPage: Br'er Rabbit. Sentence: The Walt Disney Company later adapted this character for its 1946 animated motion picture \"Song of the South\".\nPage: Tar-Baby. Sentence: The Tar-Baby is the second of the Uncle Remus stories published in 1880; it is about a doll made of tar and turpentine used by the villainous Br'er Fox to entrap Br'er Rabbit.",
